        title: "Vulnerability Assessment and Tools",
        content: 'Last lesson covered the mindset and legal foundation of ethical hacking, plus the professional frameworks that structure a penetration test. This lesson gets hands-on: the actual tools ethical hackers use every day, and the methodology that organizes how they\'re used.\n\n## Vulnerability Assessment vs. Penetration Test\n\nThese two terms get confused constantly, and the difference matters.\n\nA **vulnerability assessment** identifies, categorizes, and prioritizes weaknesses in a system — but typically stops there. It tells you "this system has a vulnerability that could allow unauthorized access," without necessarily proving it by exploiting it.\n\nA **penetration test** goes further, actively attempting to exploit identified vulnerabilities to demonstrate real-world impact — showing not just that a door might be unlocked, but that someone can actually walk through it and reach something valuable.\n\nBoth are useful, and often used together. Many organizations run frequent, lighter-weight vulnerability assessments — sometimes monthly, sometimes continuous — and reserve full penetration tests for less frequent, deeper engagements, often required annually for compliance reasons.\n\n## The Methodology\n\nA typical vulnerability assessment follows four consistent steps: **asset discovery**, identifying exactly what systems exist on a network; **vulnerability scanning**, comparing those systems against known vulnerability databases; **analysis and prioritization**, since not every vulnerability carries equal risk — an unpatched flaw on an isolated test server matters far less than the same flaw on a system holding customer payment data; and finally **remediation guidance**, providing clear, actionable steps for fixing what was found.\n\nThis is exactly the work you\'ll do in this week\'s practical exercise, using Nessus or OpenVAS — two of the most widely used vulnerability scanning platforms in the industry, both with free versions well suited to learning.\n\n## Three Tools, Three Jobs\n\n**Nmap**, short for Network Mapper, is what you\'ll use for scanning. It discovers what devices exist on a network, what ports are open on each, and often what specific software and version is running behind each open port. A simple Nmap scan might reveal a server has port 22 open, running an outdated SSH version with a known, documented vulnerability — an immediate next step for both an attacker and a defender.\n\n**Metasploit** is a penetration testing framework — a large, organized collection of known exploits, paired with tools to deliver and execute them against a target. Where Nmap tells you a vulnerability might exist, Metasploit is often used to actually demonstrate that it can be exploited. It\'s powerful, and precisely because it\'s powerful, using it outside an authorized engagement is a serious criminal offense.\n\n**Burp Suite** specializes in testing web applications. It works as an intercepting proxy, sitting between your browser and the web application you\'re testing, letting you see and even modify the actual requests being sent — extremely useful for finding issues like broken authentication or the kind of vulnerabilities described in the OWASP Top 10.\n\nNone of these three tools is inherently illegal — network administrators use the exact same tools to check their own systems. **Authorization is what determines whether the use is ethical, not the tool itself.**\n\n## What\'s Next\n\nFinding a vulnerability is only half the job. Next lesson covers the other half: how to report what you found clearly enough that it actually gets fixed, and the principle of responsible disclosure.',
